  "start": 6834.94,
  "end": 6993.34,
  "text": "Hello. Good evening, council members. I'm Jennifer DeSato. I'm the executive director of the Oshar Life Law, where it is to George Mason University and chairman of the fall for the book festival of our board of directors. Fall for the book launched in 1998, as Northern Virginia's oldest literary festival has been supported by the city of Fairfax, its inception, and that's nearly 30 years. The annual fall event spanning several days attracts approximately 10,000 people, attendees with a diverse lineup of free and ticketed programming. Our entire festival is free and open to the public. This includes authors, readings, panel discussions, writing workshops, and children's activities, featuring prominent writers like Amy Tan, Colson Whitehead, Steven King, and David Graham, alongside emerging authors, and national authors. Events are held across Fairfax locations, such as library, storage meetings, and use of university and civic spaces. This festival operates through a partnership with the city of Fairfax, George Mason University, and Fairfax County Public Library. The city contributes $25,000 annually, funding operational costs like venue rentals, author fees, and marketing. This investment generates an estimated $225,000 in local revenue, a ninefold return based on attendee spending at Fairfax restaurants, hotels, shops, and track through economic impact assessments. George Mason University provides campus facilities and staffing, well Fairfax County Public Libraries, offer additional venues and coordinates, community outreach supplemented by grants. Beyond the fall festival fall for the book, delivers year-round literary engagement across Fairfax. This includes 15 to 20 additional events, such as author talks, hosted at Old Town Hall, the Sherwood Center, Libraries, and George Mason University, writing workshops for teens and adults in spring and summer, and youth reading programs like Literacy Outreach. With Fairfax County Public Schools, these efforts reach an additional 2000 to 3000 more participants annually fostering ongoing education and cultural participation."
